In Shiny, inputs that are vectors (for example selectInput(..., multiple = TRUE) or a group of checkboxes) are available as a single reactive value (a vector). observeEvent can watch that reactive value directly — it will fire whenever the vector changes. The main points are:

- observeEvent(eventExpr, handlerExpr) takes the reactive expression to watch in eventExpr (e.g. input$myVec). For vector inputs this is just input$myVec.
- When the vector is empty it may be NULL or length 0 (depends on control). Use is.null() or length() to test emptiness.
- If you need to compare the new vector to the previous one (detect additions/removals), store the previous value (e.g. in a reactiveVal) and compare using identical(), setequal(), or setdiff().

Examples

1) Basic observeEvent for a multiple select:
```r
ui <- fluidPage(
  selectInput("choices", "Pick:", choices = letters[1:6], multiple = TRUE),
  verbatimTextOutput("out")
)

server <- function(input, output, session) {
  observeEvent(input$choices, {
    # input$choices is a character vector (or NULL if nothing selected)
    if (is.null(input$choices) || length(input$choices) == 0) {
      message("No choices selected")
    } else {
      message("Selected: ", paste(input$choices, collapse = ", "))
    }
  }, ignoreNULL = FALSE)  # ignoreNULL = FALSE makes it run when it becomes NULL
}
```

2) Detect what items were added or removed:
```r
server <- function(input, output, session) {
  previous <- reactiveVal(character(0))

  observeEvent(input$choices, {
    old <- previous()
    new <- input$choices %||% character(0)  # %||% from shiny or use if (is.null(...)) ...
    added <- setdiff(new, old)
    removed <- setdiff(old, new)

    if (length(added)) message("Added: ", paste(added, collapse = ", "))
    if (length(removed)) message("Removed: ", paste(removed, collapse = ", "))

    previous(new)
  }, ignoreNULL = FALSE)
}
```

Notes and tips
- ignoreNULL = TRUE (default) prevents the handler from running when the event expression becomes NULL. Set ignoreNULL = FALSE if you want to handle deselection (NULL) cases.
- If you want the handler to run once at startup even if input hasn't changed, set ignoreInit = FALSE.
- If you need to watch multiple inputs together, use eventExpr = reactive({ list(input$a, input$b, input$vec) }) or wrap them in list() in the eventExpr.
- Comparing unordered selections: use setequal() if order doesn't matter.

If you tell me what input control you’re using (selectInput, checkboxGroupInput, fileInput, etc.) and what behavior you want (just detect changes, detect added/removed items, treat NULL as empty), I can give a tailored example.

